sections.s2_technical:
A request DTO models what a client may send. It carries validation annotations like NotBlank, Email, and Size, so invalid input is rejected before any business logic runs. Critically, it includes only client-settable fields. It must not include server-controlled fields like id, createdAt, balance, or role, because if those were present and bound to the entity, a client could set them, which is the mass-assignment vulnerability.

```java
// Request DTO: client-settable fields only, with validation. No id, no balance, no role.
public record CreateUserRequest(
        @NotBlank String name,
        @Email String email,
        @NotBlank @Size(min = 8) String password) {}
```

A response DTO models what a client may receive. It omits secrets like the password entirely, and includes server-generated fields like id and createdAt that make sense to return. Because input and output differ, a single shared DTO would either leak a password on the way out or carry fields a client should not set on the way in. Separate DTOs make each direction exactly right.

```java
// Response DTO: safe output fields only. No password.
public record UserResponse(String id, String name, String email, Instant createdAt) {}
```

The reason this matters most is security. If you reuse one DTO for both, the password field exists on the response, risking a leak, and server fields exist on the request, risking mass-assignment. Separate DTOs give each direction a minimal, correct surface. Validation also differs by direction: the request needs validation; the response does not, because the server produced it.

The second question is where conversion lives. It belongs in the service layer. The controller should be a thin adapter that receives a request DTO, delegates to the service, and returns a response DTO. The service performs the mapping from request DTO to entity, runs the business logic, and maps the entity to a response DTO. Keeping mapping in the service means the logic and its data shaping live together, can be unit tested without a web layer, and are reusable by other callers like a message consumer or a scheduled job that are not HTTP controllers.

```java
@Service
public class UserService {
    public UserResponse create(CreateUserRequest req) {
        User entity = new User(generateId(), req.name(), req.email(), hash(req.password()));
        repo.save(entity);
        return toResponse(entity); // mapping here, in the service
    }
    private UserResponse toResponse(User u) {
        return new UserResponse(u.getId(), u.getName(), u.getEmail(), u.getCreatedAt());
    }
}
```

If mapping lived in the controller, every entry point would reimplement it, drift apart, and a non-HTTP caller could not reuse it. Putting it in the service centralises it. For larger projects a dedicated mapper, such as a MapStruct interface invoked by the service, keeps it clean, but the invocation still belongs to the service layer, not the controller.

A senior nuance: the controller should never see the entity at all. It speaks only DTOs. This keeps the web layer ignorant of persistence, so you could swap the data store or add a second transport like gRPC and reuse the same service and mapping. The separation of request versus response DTOs plus service-layer mapping is a core part of clean layering that interviewers use to gauge whether you understand boundaries rather than just moving data around.

